\label{def:oriented-dyadic-braid-group}
    The \emph{oriented dyadic braid group} $B_{\vec{F}}$ has braid generators $\sigma_{ab}$ for any two dyadic rational numbers $a<b$ in $(0,1)$, and $\vec{F}$ generators $g_i$ for $i \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$, with the following relations:
    \begin{align*}
        \text{braid relations: }&\sigma_{ab}\sigma_{cd}=\sigma_{cd}\sigma_{ab} \ \text{ if $(a-c)(a-d)(b-c)(b-d)>0$,} \\
        &\sigma_{ab}\sigma_{bc}=\sigma_{ac}\sigma_{ab}=\sigma_{bc}\sigma_{ac} \ \text{  for all $a,b,c$ with $a<b<c$}. \\ \text{$\vec{F}$ relations: } &g_n g_l = g_l g_{n+2} \ \text{ for all $l<n$} \\ \text{braid $\vec{F}$ relations: } &g_i\sigma_{ab} = \sigma_{g_i(a)g_i(b)}g_i \ \text{ for all $i,a \text{ and } b$} 
    \end{align*}

\label{def: Thompson group}
	Thompson's group $F$ is the group (under composition) of homeomorphisms $g$ from $[0,1]$ to itself satisfying the following conditions:	
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $g$ is piecewise linear and orientation-preserving.
		\item In the pieces where $g$ is linear, the slope is always a power of $2$.
		\item The breakpoints are dyadic rational numbers in $[0,1]$, i.e. $\frac{k}{2^m}$ for some $m \in \{0,1,2,...\}$ and $k \in \{0,1,2,...,2^m\}$.
	\end{enumerate}

\label{def: s.d. partition pair representation}
	Suppose that $\mathcal{I}$ is a s.d. $n$-partition with breakpoints $0 = a_0 < a_1 < ... < a_n = 1$, and $\mathcal{J}$ is a s.d. $n$-partition with breakpoints $0 = b_0 < b_1 < ... < b_n = 1$. We define $\gamma^{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{J}}: [0,1] \to [0,1]$ to be the piecewise linear function determined by letting $\gamma^{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{J}}(a_i)=b_i$ for all $i \in \{0,1,...,n\}$ and letting $\gamma^{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{J}}$ be linear on $[a_i,a_{i+1}]$ for all $i$.

	Note that $\gamma^\mathcal{I}_\mathcal{J}$ is an element in $F$. We say $(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{J})$ is a pair of s.d. partitions representing $\gamma^\mathcal{I}_\mathcal{J}$. It is well known that every element in $F$ can be represented by some pair of s.d. partitions.

\label{def: Pi_J}
