Thus we may assume there exists some $i\neq 1$ such that $\sigma_i = -$. Then we prove the statement inductively. The base case is the sequence $(+, -)$, for which we take the tree with a vertex on the right edge of the root. Assume that the statement holds for all sequences of length less than $n$. For any sequence $(\sigma_1, \sigma_2,\cdots, \sigma_n)$, let $j$ be the smallest number such that $\sigma_j = -$. Note that $(-\sigma_j, -\sigma_{j+1}, ..., -\sigma_n)$ is a sequence of length shorter than $n$, with the first sign $+$. We attach its corresponding tree to the right edge of $T_{j-1}^+$, then the resulting tree is the desired one.

\label{prop:two-numbers-to-any-ab}
    For any pair of dyadic numbers $a < b$, there exists $f \in \vec{F}$ such that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $f(\frac{1}{4}) = a$ and $f(\frac{1}{2})=b$, if $s(a)=s(b)=+$.
        \item $f(\frac{1}{2}) = a$ and $f(\frac{3}{4})=b$, if $s(a)=+, s(b)=-$.
        \item $f(\frac{5}{8}) = a$ and $f(\frac{3}{4})=b$, if $s(a)=s(b)=-$.
        \item $f(\frac{3}{4}) = a$ and $f(\frac{7}{8})=b$, if $s(a)=-, s(b)=+$.
    \end{itemize}

We start with the first case that $s(a) = s(b) = +$. It suffices to find two trees $T^{up}$ with vertices $\{v_1^{up}<\cdots<v_n^{up}\}$  and $T^{down}$ with vertices $\{v_1^{down}<\cdots<v_n^{down}\}$ satisfying the following: 
    \begin{enumerate} 
        \item The sign $s(v_k^{up})=s(v_k^{down})$ for each $1\leq k \leq n$.
        \item There exist $1 \leq i,j \leq n$ such that $v_i^{up}=\frac{1}{4}$, $v_j^{up}=\frac{1}{2}$ and $v_i^{down}=a$, $v_j^{down}=b$.
    \end{enumerate}

Then this pair of trees $(T^{up}, T^{down})$ gives us an element $f \in \vec{F}$ sending $\frac{1}{4}$ to $a$ and sending $\frac{1}{2}$ to $b$. We will use the above two lemmas to construct these two trees. 

We let $T^{down}$ be the tree given by Lemma \ref{lem:lower-tree}, such that $v_i^{down} = a, v_j^{down} = b$ and $s(v_1^{down})=+, s(v_2^{down})=-, s(v_{i+1}^{down})=-, s(v_{j+1}^{down})=-$. Consider the three sub-sequences separated by $v_i^{down}$ and $v_j^{down}$. They induce three sequences of signs
    \begin{align*}
        & \Sigma_1 = \{s(v_1^{down}) = +, s(v_2^{down}) = -, \cdots, s(v_{i-1}^{down})\}\\
        & \Sigma_2 = \{s(v_{i+1}^{down}) = -, s(v_{i+2}^{down}), \cdots, s(v_{j-1}^{down})\} \\
        & \Sigma_3 = \{s(v_{j+1}^{down}) = -, s(v_{j+2}^{down}), \cdots, s(v_n^{down})\}
    \end{align*}

    By Lemma \ref{lem:upper-trees}, we can find a tree $T_1^{up}$ inducing $\Sigma_1$, a tree $T_2^{up}$ inducing $-\Sigma_2$ (because $s(v_{i+1}^{down}) = -$), and a tree $T_3^{up}$ inducing $-\Sigma_3$ (because $s(v_{j+1}^{down}) = -$). Let $T^{up}$ be the tree constructed from $T_1^{up}, T_2^{up}$ and $T_3^{up}$ as Figure \ref{fig:tree-plus-plus} shows. It is not hard to check that the pair $(T^{up}, T^{down})$ satisfies the above two conditions.

