        \end{subfigure}
        \caption{Trees (A), (B), (C) and (D) correspond to the four possible sign combinations of the points $a$ and $b$.}
    \end{figure}

    The second case can be proved similarly, but with $T_3^{up}$ inducing $\Sigma_3$ instead (because $s(v_{j+1}^{down}) = +$). The tree $T^{up}$ is constructed from $T_1^{up}, T_2^{up}$ and $T_3^{up}$ as Figure \ref{fig:tree-plus-minus} shows.

    The proof for the third and fourth cases is slightly different from the first two. After we determine the tree $T^{down}$, we let the first sequence of signs instead be
    $$
    \Sigma_1' = \{s(v_2^{down}) = -, \cdots, s(v_{i-1}^{down})\}
    $$
    without $s(v_1^{down})$. Then consider the three sequences $\Sigma_1', \Sigma_2$ and $\Sigma_3$.

    In the third case, we find a tree $T_1^{up}$ inducing $-\Sigma_1'$, a tree $T_2^{up}$ inducing $\Sigma_2$ and a tree $T_3^{up}$ inducing $\Sigma_3$. The tree $T^{up}$ is given by Figure \ref{fig:tree-minus-plus}.

    In the fourth case, the tree $T_3^{up}$ induces $-\Sigma_3$ instead, and the tree $T^{up}$ is given by Figure \ref{fig:tree-minus-minus}. This completes the proof.
